1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,800
 So in this lecture, we're going to discuss how inference parameters influence foundation model behavior.

2
00:00:06,800 --> 00:00:10,600
 But what are inference parameters?

3
00:00:10,600 --> 00:00:15,000
 So earlier when we had discussed how does Amazon bedrock work?

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:18,600
 We said user input is who is the best tennis player?

5
00:00:18,600 --> 00:00:26,200
 And then we said we're going to make this API request to bedrock with some configuration parameters where we identified the model ID.

6
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:33,500
 So this is the model ID to which the request should be sent to then we sent the prompt, which is the user input.

7
00:00:33,500 --> 00:00:38,100
 And then along with that, we also sent some configuration parameters.

8
00:00:38,100 --> 00:00:43,300
 So we said, okay, max token is going to 512, temperature is going to be 0.5.

9
00:00:43,300 --> 00:00:51,600
 So these configuration parameters that you sent along with the prompt are called as inference parameters.

10
00:00:52,000 --> 00:01:01,500
 So there are broadly two kind of inference parameters and you might see some variation in a few models, but generally speaking, these are the two main ones.

11
00:01:01,500 --> 00:01:16,600
 So the first one is randomness and diversity and this controls the creativity of the response that you're going to get from the foundation model and we'll get to what we mean by creativity of the response in a little bit.

12
00:01:17,400 --> 00:01:25,300
 But from the randomness and diversity control perspective, there are three key attributes that you can control temperature, top key, and top key.

13
00:01:25,300 --> 00:01:27,800
 Then the second one is the length.

14
00:01:27,800 --> 00:01:34,100
 So the length of the output that is generated by the foundation model based on your prompt.

15
00:01:34,100 --> 00:01:42,300
 So there are two ways in which you can control the length of the response, one is max tokens and seconds the stop sequence.

16
00:01:42,300 --> 00:01:45,900
 Okay, now let's try and understand this in more detail through an example.

17
00:01:46,100 --> 00:01:49,200
 So the first inference parameter is randomness and diversity.

18
00:01:49,200 --> 00:02:00,800
 So let's say the prompt we're going to send to the foundation model is I hear the hoof beats of and our foundation model is going to complete this sentence.

19
00:02:00,800 --> 00:02:08,200
 So we know that foundation models generate one word at a time to complete the sentence.

20
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:18,100
 Now to complete the next word in this sentence foundation model may come up with hundreds of words that can come after off here.

21
00:02:18,100 --> 00:02:23,300
 In our case, let's just assume that it comes up with five words.

22
00:02:23,300 --> 00:02:29,100
 So first it comes up with horse and it gives it a probability score of 0.4.

23
00:02:29,100 --> 00:02:33,200
 Then it says win and it gives a probability score of 0.2.

24
00:02:33,400 --> 00:02:40,500
 Maybe unicorns with 0.1 probability score and change in distance, which is 0.05.

25
00:02:40,500 --> 00:02:49,400
 Okay, now let's see through temperature, top can, top P, how we can control what is the next word that would be generated.

26
00:02:49,400 --> 00:02:54,900
 So for temperature, different foundation models would have a different range.

27
00:02:54,900 --> 00:02:58,400
 Maybe some will have 0 to 5, some may have 0 to 1.

28
00:02:58,800 --> 00:03:03,400
 So for our use case, let's just assume the values between 0 to 1.

29
00:03:03,400 --> 00:03:11,600
 Okay, now if you have a low value, which is closer to 0, then model tends to select higher probability words.

30
00:03:11,600 --> 00:03:17,700
 And if you see in this scenario, then the word with highest probability is horse 0.4.

31
00:03:17,700 --> 00:03:21,300
 So it's going to come up with this word horse.

32
00:03:21,300 --> 00:03:25,800
 That means it's a highly predictable response.

33
00:03:26,600 --> 00:03:34,900
 So now if you want to be really creative, then the temperature value we can have for our foundation model is let's say one or closer to one.

34
00:03:34,900 --> 00:03:43,400
 And when we provide a higher value, temperature further away from 0, the model may select a lower probability word.

35
00:03:43,400 --> 00:03:48,800
 And in our case, the word with lowest probability was change in distance.

36
00:03:48,800 --> 00:03:53,300
 So the next word, it's going to come up with this change in distance.

37
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:59,400
 So next we have top K and it can have value between 1 to 500 and it may vary from model to model.

38
00:03:59,400 --> 00:04:01,600
 So what does top K do?

39
00:04:01,600 --> 00:04:06,500
 The cut off where the model no longer selects the words.

40
00:04:06,500 --> 00:04:18,400
 So let's say in our case, obviously we have only five words here, but let's say if we put K is equal to 50, the model selects from 50 of the most probable words.

41
00:04:19,200 --> 00:04:29,600
 So now in our example, let's say if we put the value of K equal to three, so it's only going to select the top three words, horse, wind and unicorn.

42
00:04:29,600 --> 00:04:33,500
 And now let's say our temperature value was equal to one.

43
00:04:33,500 --> 00:04:41,200
 Now, since only these three words are selected, so the word we have here would be unicorn and not change in distance.

44
00:04:41,200 --> 00:04:48,100
 So that's how you can use a combination of temperature and top K to determine the response from the foundation model.

45
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:53,200
 Then we have top P and these values can vary from model to model.

46
00:04:53,200 --> 00:04:55,400
 So but what does top K do?

47
00:04:55,400 --> 00:04:59,100
 It caps choices based on the sum of the probabilities.

48
00:04:59,100 --> 00:05:03,100
 So let's say in our example, you have the top P value as 0.6.

49
00:05:03,100 --> 00:05:07,200
 So what it will do is it will add the probability of first two words.

50
00:05:07,200 --> 00:05:08,500
 So we have horse and wind.

51
00:05:08,500 --> 00:05:11,500
 So it's going to only select these two words.

52
00:05:11,500 --> 00:05:16,400
 And let's say if you have low value of temperature, then it's going to select again, horse, so should not change much.

53
00:05:16,700 --> 00:05:24,600
 But let's say if you select a higher value, then it's going to select this word, wind and not change in distance.

54
00:05:24,600 --> 00:05:34,200
 So that's how you can use a combination of temperature, top K and top P to determine the creative response from the model.

55
00:05:34,200 --> 00:05:38,600
 Now, let's go and understand the second inference parameter, which is length.

56
00:05:38,600 --> 00:05:44,600
 Now, let's see, we provide a prompt to the foundation model where we see right an essay on horse.

57
00:05:45,500 --> 00:05:47,000
 So how does the foundation model know?

58
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:51,900
 How many words to generate if we have not provided any guidance in the prompt?

59
00:05:51,900 --> 00:05:55,900
 So that's where the inference parameter length comes into click.

60
00:05:55,900 --> 00:05:59,900
 So it controls the length of the generated response.

61
00:05:59,900 --> 00:06:05,800
 So not only does it keep the response concise, but it also helps control cost.

62
00:06:05,800 --> 00:06:12,400
 Just remember the more the number of words, the more the number of output tokens, more is going to be the cost.

63
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:17,300
 Now the length in the parameter can be controlled by max length and stop sequence.

64
00:06:17,300 --> 00:06:23,700
 So max length specify is the maximum number of tokens to use in the generated response.

65
00:06:23,700 --> 00:06:26,300
 So now remember, it's the max number of tokens.

66
00:06:26,300 --> 00:06:33,700
 It can also generate lesser number of words or tokens, but once you provide the max length, it cannot go beyond that.

67
00:06:33,700 --> 00:06:38,600
 And that can vary between one to 64,000 and it varies from model to model.

68
00:06:38,800 --> 00:06:45,600
 And 64,000 is maximum, which is in the case of entropic claw model.

69
00:06:45,600 --> 00:06:50,500
 So if you see our example previously, where we had sent the API request to them is in bedrock.

70
00:06:50,500 --> 00:06:57,800
 Here we had this max underscore token attribute and we had said 512 tokens and

71
00:06:57,800 --> 00:07:01,200
 512 tokens approximately 375 words or so.

72
00:07:01,200 --> 00:07:06,800
 So which means once it generates an essay on horse, it cannot be more than 375 words.

73
00:07:06,900 --> 00:07:08,600
 It can be less, but not more than that.

74
00:07:08,600 --> 00:07:12,700
 Now the second one is stop secans.

75
00:07:12,700 --> 00:07:18,900
 So now it's clear to us that it cannot generate more than whatever token limit that we have provided.

76
00:07:18,900 --> 00:07:25,500
 But how does it know when to stop and there are three ways in which it can be done.

77
00:07:25,500 --> 00:07:28,900
 So the first one is model decided stop.

78
00:07:28,900 --> 00:07:36,800
 So based on the model training, it automatically determines what could be the appropriate length for a essay like this.

79
00:07:36,900 --> 00:07:44,200
 So that's the first one, the second one is system and force, which is when you are sending the inference parameters to the model.

80
00:07:44,200 --> 00:07:46,800
 And here you can see max tokens.

81
00:07:46,800 --> 00:07:51,300
 So if you provide the max token length, then we already discussed it's not going to exceed that.

82
00:07:51,300 --> 00:07:53,900
 So the third one is stop secans.

83
00:07:53,900 --> 00:07:57,500
 So in stop secans, you can provide some keywords.

84
00:07:57,500 --> 00:08:03,800
 So you provide this closed curly bracket or you provide a tag answer, as you can see in this case.

85
00:08:04,600 --> 00:08:06,900
 So what's the relevance this now let's take an example.

86
00:08:06,900 --> 00:08:15,100
 Let's say now let's say you have built an insurance generator application where you get the claim form you extract the data from the claim form.

87
00:08:15,100 --> 00:08:21,300
 Then you do some formatting of that extracted data and then you persist in the backend system.

88
00:08:21,300 --> 00:08:23,300
 Maybe RDS, DynamoDB and so on.

89
00:08:23,300 --> 00:08:32,700
 Now let's say our DynamoDB accepts the data in this format as a JSON object where we have the first name John and last name as do.

90
00:08:33,600 --> 00:08:45,100
 So what will happen is when we have the stop secans in the format of curly brackets, then it does a match and when he sees in the response closed curly bracket.

91
00:08:45,100 --> 00:08:50,900
 It will stop generating any further tokens post that and how is that useful.

92
00:08:50,900 --> 00:08:58,700
 So if it generates any more tokens post that curly bracket, then it will not match to the backend schema.

93
00:08:59,200 --> 00:09:03,700
 So that is one of the scenarios in which you can use stop secans.

94
00:09:03,700 --> 00:09:07,800
 Okay, so that's pretty much it from this lecture as in the next lecture.

